<exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-cross-product">
<title>Computing cross product</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li>
			Use the <xref ref="equation-orthog-concepts-cross-prod-det-formula" text="custom">determinant method</xref> to compute <m> \ucrossprod{u}{v} </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Use the dot product to verify that your computed answer for <m> \ucrossprod{u}{v} </m> is
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthogonal" text="custom">orthogonal</xref></term> to each of <m> \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \uvec{v} </m>.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = ( 1,1,2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = (-3,1,4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m>
		\ucrossprod{u}{v} =
		\begin{avmatrix}{rrr}
			\ivec{} \amp \jvec{} \amp \kvec{} \\
			 1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
			-3 \amp 1 \amp 4
		\end{avmatrix}
		= (2, -10, 4)
		</m></li>

		<li><p>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{u} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (1,1,2) }{ (2,-10,4) }
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 1 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot (-10) + 2 \cdot 4
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{v} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (-3,1,4) }{ (2,-10,4) }
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = -3 \cdot 2 + 1 \cdot (-10) + 4 \cdot 4
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = (-3,1,4) </m>
		<m> \uvec{v} = ( 1,1,2) </m>,
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m>
		\ucrossprod{u}{v} =
		\begin{avmatrix}{rrr}
			\ivec{} \amp \jvec{} \amp \kvec{} \\
			-3 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\
			 1 \amp 1 \amp 2
		\end{avmatrix}
		= (-2, 10, -4)
		</m></li>

		<li><p>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{u} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (-3,1,4) }{ (-2,10,-4) }
					\amp = (-3) \cdot (-2) + 1 \cdot (10) + 4 \cdot (-4)
				</mrow><mrow>
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{v} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (1,1,2) }{ (-2,10,-4) }
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 1 \cdot (-2) + 1 \cdot 10 + 2 \cdot (-4)
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = ( 2,2,4) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = (-3,1,4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m>
		\ucrossprod{u}{v} =
		\begin{avmatrix}{rrr}
			\ivec{} \amp \jvec{} \amp \kvec{} \\
			 2 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\
			-3 \amp 1 \amp 4
		\end{avmatrix}
		= (4, -20, 8)
		</m></li>

		<li><p>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{u} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (2,2,4) }{ (4,-20,8) }
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 2 \cdot 4 + 2 \cdot (-20) + 4 \cdot 8
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

			<md>
				<mrow>
					\dotprod{ \uvec{v} }{( \ucrossprod{u}{v} )}
					\amp = \dotprod{ (-3,1,4) }{ (4,-20,8) }
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = -3 \cdot 4 + 1 \cdot (-20) + 4 \cdot 8
				</mrow><mrow>
					\amp = 0
				</mrow>
			</md>

		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> \uvec{u} = (1,1,2) </m>,
		<m> \uvec{v} = (2,2,4) </m>
	</p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><m>
		\ucrossprod{u}{v} =
		\begin{vmatrix}
			\ivec{} \amp \jvec{} \amp \kvec{} \\
			1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
			2 \amp 2 \amp 4
		\end{vmatrix}
		= (0,0,0)
		</m></li>

		<li><p> Clearly both <m> \dotprod{ \uvec{u} }{ \zerovec } = 0 </m> and <m> \dotprod{ \uvec{v} }{ \zerovec } = 0 </m>. </p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
